==English==

===Etymology===
From late 17th century {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|effronterie||lang=fr}}, from {{term|effronté||shameless, insolent|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|esfronté||lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|VL.|en}} {{m|la|*exfrontātus}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|effrons||barefaced}}, from the prefix {{term|ex-||from|lang=la}} + {{m|la|frōns||forehead}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪˈfɹʌntəɹi/|/ɛˈfɹʌntəɹi/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|effronteries}}

# {{context|uncountable|lang=en}} [[insolent|Insolent]] and [[shameless]] [[audacity]].
#: ''We even had the '''effrontery''' to suggest that he should leave the country.''
# {{context|countable|lang=en}} An [[act]] of insolent and shameless audacity.
#: ''Any refusal to salute the president shall be counted as an '''effrontery'''.''

